; Format 3: the original segment. One book of the UHB or the UGNT, the same for every language. A word's position is
; its index in "words" (ids.md).

orig-segment = body<orig-header, orig-tables>

orig-header = {
  "class": "orig",
  "v": 1,
  "tables": { * table-name => [uint, bool] },
  "book": book-code,
  "orig": [tstr, tstr],        ; this book's own key and stamp: what other segments name
}

orig-tables = {
  "words": orig-words,         ; required, v1
  "text": orig-text,           ; required, v1
  ? "macula": macula-v1 / macula-v2,   ; optional; the version in h.tables says which
}

orig-words = {
  "form": strtab,              ; distinct values of each attribute, most used first, ties by code point
  "lemma": strtab,
  "strong": strtab,
  "morph": strtab,
  "cols": {                    ; per word, in book order: the value's number in its table
    "form": uvarints,
    "lemma": uvarints,
    "strong": uvarints,
    "morph": uvarints,
  },
  ? "other": [* [word: uint, { * tstr => tstr }]],   ; any other attribute of a word, as published
}
; A word without one of the four attributes has the empty string "" there; every table holds "" when a word uses it.
; Strong numbers are kept as published ("H7462b", "b:H4999", "G54850"); ids.md says how a map key is made from them.

orig-text = {
  "seps": strtab,              ; what lies between words, and every structure argument; most used first
  "tok": uvarints,             ; per piece: 0 for the next word, n for separator n - 1
  "ev": uvarints,              ; events as in the book segment's texts; arguments are numbers in "seps"
  ? "attrs": [* [event: uint, { * tstr => tstr }]],
}
; Words inside footnotes count like any other word, in document order. The ketiv and qere marks are kept as
; published (as nodes, or as attributes in "other").

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; MACULA (Biblica, CC BY 4.0: Clear-Bible/macula-hebrew and macula-greek, the lowfat trees and the referent columns).
; Two versions of the optional table. The version is the number in h.tables: [1, false] or [2, false]. A reader that
; knows only v1 skips v2, and the other way round (container.md: an optional table of an unknown version is skipped), so
; the original opens without MACULA. The builder writes v2 only; a reader of this specification reads both.
; `release` and the license of the resource in the catalog are the same in both versions.

macula-v1 = {
  "release": tstr,             ; the MACULA release the data comes from
  ? "trees": any,              ; v1: the value published today (clause trees per verse)
  ? "referents": any,          ; v1: the value published today (who each pronoun refers to)
}
; v1 keeps MACULA as it is published, under its own license, and stays as it is. It holds the data of Taller's files, one
; per book (`src/domain/syntaxTree.ts`, `src/domain/referents.ts`, built by `scripts/build-trees.mts` and
; `scripts/build-referents.mts`): Taller is the source of every code below. Phase 0 measured it at 193 KB for Psalms
; after compression; for Genesis it is 564 925 of the 1 017 315 body bytes of `orig` (trees 307 686, referents 257 177),
; as many small nested arrays. No reader uses the contents of v1: the output of a reader gives the value as it is
; (tests/README.md, `macula.v` 1). The codes of v1, which v2 keeps in another layout:
;
;   referents  {"words": [[form, strong]], "verses": {"C:V": [[key, occurrence, [target, ...]], ...]}}
;     A row of "verses" is a word of that verse that refers to others: `key` is its referentKey and `occurrence` its
;     occurrence (both defined below). A target is [kind, piece, chapter, verse, word]:
;       kind     "r" the word refers: a pronoun, or the suffix of a Hebrew word, stands for the target;
;                "s" subject left unsaid: who a verb speaks of when its sentence does not name them and MACULA gives the
;                    verb no frame (MACULA's subjref column);
;                "c" causer: the place `AA` of the verb's frame;
;                "a" agent: the place `A0`;
;                "p" patient: the place `A1`;
;                "o" any other place of the frame.
;       piece    the suffix's text when only a piece of a Hebrew word refers; "" when the whole word does;
;       chapter, verse   the verse of the target word, in the numbering of the app's texts; 0, 0 is the verse of the
;                word that refers;
;       word     a number in "words": [form, strong], the target as pointed text without the marks of the chant, and its
;                Strong (`H3124`, `G2424`; "" when MACULA gives none).
;     Source of the meanings. Frame places: macula-hebrew documentation, section 2.1, attribute Frame: "A0 indicates
;     arguments exhibiting features of prototypical Agents. A1 ... prototypical Patients. AA indicates Agents of induced
;     actions--cause A0 to act". Implied subject: the same section, attribute SubjRef ("the implied subject of a verb").
;     Pronoun referent: the same section, the Ref attribute ("the nearest nominal antecedent of a pronoun"). The Greek
;     manual does not describe these columns (it has no Frame, SubjRef or referent attribute in its text); the Greek
;     table is read by Taller with the same rules. That "o" is "any other place of the frame", that "s" and "r" are left
;     out when the frame already names the same word, and that a frame place filled by a pronoun gives what the pronoun
;     stands for, are Taller's rules (`referentFiles`), not MACULA's.
;
;   trees     {"sentences": [tree, ...], "words": {"C:V": n}}
;     A tree is [label, child, ...]. A child is a tree, a leaf (the number CCCVVVWWW: chapter, verse and word of the
;     verse, from 1) or, when only a piece of a Hebrew word has the function, [CCCVVVWWW, text].
;     A label is a role followed by `*` when the part is a clause: "s", "v*", "o*", "*". The bare "*" is a clause with
;     no role. "&" is a word that joins (lowfat class `cj` or `conj` with no role): an "and" between two clauses or two
;     nouns, a "because" before a clause. The roles are the `role` attribute of MACULA's lowfat trees (list below).
;     The leaf ids are of the UHB or UGNT, fitted to the words of this book by their letters (Taller, `fitWords`).
;
; The key and the occurrence of a word (both versions).
;   referentKey(text): the text in NFD; every mark removed (\p{M}); in lower case; the final sigma ς written σ; only the
;     letters kept (\p{L}). The key of a Hebrew word is the key of the text of all its pieces together; for a word of
;     "words" it is the key of its "form".
;   occurrence: the place of a word, counted from 0, among the words of its verse that have that key, in book order.
;   v1 finds the word that refers by its verse, key and occurrence; v2 writes its position.
;
; The codes of the roles (a label is one of these, or "&", with or without `*`). The names are those of the MACULA
; documentation: Greek, "Documentation for the MACULA Greek Treebank for the Nestle 1904 Greek New Testament" (Tan and
; Wu, 2022-03-31), section 2.4; Hebrew, the macula-hebrew treebank documentation, section 2.4. The manuals write the
; labels in capitals (S, V, ADV); lowfat writes them in lower case. A role the manuals do not define is said so.
;   s     Subject function: the agent of typically transitive verbs and the single argument of intransitive verbs.  [Greek S, Hebrew S]
;   v     Verbal function: the action or event of a proposition; in a verbal clause, the head of the clause.         [Greek V, Hebrew V]
;   vc    Verbal copula function: a linking verb ("to be") between the subject and a predicate. Greek manual only.    [Greek VC]
;   o     Object function: the patient or goal of the action.                                                          [Greek O, Hebrew O]
;   o2    Second object function: a second object (a person and a thing, or a direct object and its complement).     [Greek O2, Hebrew O2]
;   io    Indirect object function: the intended recipient of the action. Greek manual only.                          [Greek IO]
;   p     Predicate function: an attribution or identification about the subject of a verbless clause or of one with a
;         copula.                                                                                                      [Greek P, Hebrew P]
;   adv   Adverbial function: the when, where, how or why of a proposition.                                          [Greek ADV, Hebrew ADV]
;   pp    Prepositional phrase function: a preposition with its object that says when, where, how or why. A role of the
;         Hebrew manual only (in the Greek manual `pp` is a phrase category, and such phrases are ADV).               [Hebrew PP]
;   aux   As used by MACULA lowfat; not defined in either manual. Kept as it comes.
;   oc    Object complement function (an assertion about the object), in the Hebrew manual, which says it is "planned for
;         the near future". Taller's list does not name it; the builder keeps every role as it comes
;         (`[a-z][a-z0-9]{0,5}`, what Taller accepts).
; `*` (clause): the manuals call a clause CL (in their examples and, for Greek, section 2.5). That lowfat writes it as `class="cl"` with the role beside
; it, and that a clause with no role is labelled "*", is how Taller reads lowfat: as used by MACULA lowfat; not defined in
; the manuals (they do not describe lowfat).
; `&`: Taller's own label. The manuals describe conjunctions (`conj` in Greek, `cj` in Hebrew) as words that link
; structures (Greek manual, section 3.9.1); giving them a label of their own is Taller's choice, not MACULA's.

macula-v2 = {
  "release": tstr,             ; the MACULA release the data comes from, as in v1
  ? "trees": {
    "labels": strtab,          ; every label ("*", "v", "s*", "&"...), most used first, ties by code point
    "pieces": strtab,          ; the texts of Hebrew word pieces, most used first (ties by code point)
    "lo": deltas,              ; per sentence: its lowest word position (svarint deltas, sentences in book order)
    "span": uvarints,          ; per sentence: highest position minus lowest
    "nodes": [* bstr],         ; per sentence: its tree in preorder, as uvarints (below)
  },
  ? "referents": {
    "ents": [* [tstr, tstr]],  ; [form, strong]: the form as published, the Strong as a map key (ids.md, Strong keys)
    "kinds": strtab,           ; "r", "s", "c", "a", "p", "o" (v1's codes), most used first, ties by code point
    "pieces": strtab,          ; "" and the suffix texts, most used first, ties by code point
    "cols": {                  ; one row per reference, rows by word position, then the source order
      "word": uvarints,        ; delta of the referring word's position from the row before (first from 0)
      "kind": uvarints, "piece": uvarints, "ent": uvarints,   ; numbers in "kinds", "pieces" and "ents"
      "ch": uvarints, "vs": uvarints,   ; the antecedent's chapter and verse (never 0: resolved)
      "ante": uvarints,        ; the antecedent's position + 1; 0 when the rule cannot fix it
    },
  },
}
; A tree in "nodes" is a list of uvarints t, read in preorder from the root.
;   t % 3 == 0   a part with the label t / 3 (a number in "labels"), followed by one uvarint: its number of children;
;   t % 3 == 1   a word, at the position lo + (t - 1) / 3;
;   t % 3 == 2   a piece of the word at lo + (t - 2) / 3, followed by one uvarint: its number in "pieces".
; `lo` and `span` are the sentence's own, in the same order as "nodes" (one number of each per sentence; three lists of the
; same length). A sentence's positions are all in lo .. lo + span. The list is used up exactly by one root: a tree cut
; short, with bytes left, or with a second root is E_BODY (tests/README.md). The root is a part (t % 3 == 0): a tree
; whose root is a word or a piece is E_BODY, and so is a part with 0 children. The writer writes `lo` and `span` exactly
; (the lowest and the highest leaf); a reader checks only that lo .. lo + span lies inside the book's words and that every
; leaf lies inside lo .. lo + span, not that they are exact. Positions are those of this segment's
; "words" (ids.md, Positions in the original).
;
; Builder rules (a reader checks none of them; it checks only the E_BODY list of tests/README.md).
;   - Which verse a word is in (trees and referents): the `chapter` and `verse` events of "text" are read in order. A label gives
;     its number by its leading digits (`13-14` is 13, `1a` is 1). A `chapter` event resets the verse to 0. Words before the
;     first `verse` event of a chapter are in verse 0: it has no ch:vs, so the targets there are left out (see "What is left
;     out"). A verse that appears in two runs is the first run: the words of the second run are not found by ch:vs.
;   - A row of "referents" is one reference of one word: the word at the position given by the running sum of "word",
;     the kind, the piece ("" when the whole word refers) and the entity "ents[ent]" that MACULA names for it. A word with
;     several references has several rows, in MACULA's order. Rows follow the book order of the referring word.
;   - The referring word is found in this book by its verse, its referentKey and its occurrence, as defined above: the
;     key is that of the word's "form" in "words"; the verse is the one the word is in (the `verse` events of "text"); the
;     occurrence counts from 0 among the words of that verse with that key. A word that cannot be found is counted in the
;     build report, per book, and its rows are left out. Nothing is guessed.
;   - "ch" and "vs" are the antecedent's verse in the numbering the positions are counted in (the `verse` events of the
;     book's "text"). The v1 pair 0, 0 ("the verse of the word that refers") is written as that verse, so 0 never occurs.
;   - "ante" is fixed by this rule and no other (the controller's ruling R1; it is the rule that gave the percentages of the
;     plan, 80 to 94 % of the references). The candidates are the words of the verse ch:vs whose Strong has the same
;     number as the entity's. The number is the letter H or G and its digits: for Greek the first four digits, for Hebrew
;     the number without leading zeros and without the letter suffix ("H7462b", "b:H4999" and "H4999" give H7462 and
;     H4999; "G54850" and "G5485" give G5485). A Strong with no such number (the empty one) matches nothing.
;       one candidate                       its position + 1;
;       several candidates                  keep those whose referentKey contains, or is contained in, the referentKey of the
;                                           entity's form (an empty key matches nothing); if exactly one is kept, its position + 1;
;       none, or still several               0: the row is kept and the antecedent is left empty.
;     The rows with 0 are counted in the build report, per book. A reader that gets 0 still has "ch", "vs" and the
;     entity's form and Strong to show the antecedent.
;   - "ents" holds each [form, strong] once, in the order the builder first meets them walking the published verses (not the
;     order of the written rows). The form is MACULA's text without the marks of the
;     chant, as v1's "words"; the Strong is a map key as ids.md says: the Hebrew part after the last ":" with its letter
;     suffix, the Greek in five digits; a Strong that is neither is "". The other tables ("labels", "pieces", "kinds") are
;     sorted most used first, ties by code point, counting every use in the book.
;   - What is left out: a tree leaf (a word or a piece) whose place cannot be found in this book's words, and a referring
;     word whose place cannot be found. Both are counted in the build report, per book. An antecedent the rule cannot fix is
;     left empty (0), not left out. A third case: a referent target whose verse is not valid (exactly one of x, y
;     is 0, or the resolved verse has a 0) is left out, and counted in the build report as "N reference(s) with no valid
;     verse: left out". A fourth: a sentence whose kept tree is deeper than 64 levels is left out and counted, with the
;     report line `MACULA trees: N sentence(s) deeper than 64 levels: left out of the trees`.
;   - After the leaves are dropped, a part left with no word or piece is dropped too, recursively (a part's children are
;     its remaining parts and leaves; a part with none goes), and it is counted with the sentence's dropped leaves. A
;     sentence left with no word or piece is dropped and counted. This is done before `lo` and `span` are computed, so a tree
;     never has a part with 0 children.
